<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Castile</h1><p class="st-meta">850 CE – 1065 CE (approx.) · capital Burgos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The frontier county of the Kingdom of León, made autonomous by Fernán González, that grew into the Kingdom of Castile.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, 'land of castles', began in the 9th century as a frontier district of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias</a> in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a> region. Its first known count, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rodrigo-of-castile">Rodrigo</a>, governed from around 850, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-rodriguez-porcelos">Diego Rodríguez Porcelos</a> founded Burgos in 884. Several local counts governed until the territory was united.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernan-gonzalez-of-castile">Fernán González</a>, from about 931 to 970, gathered the Castilian counties and made his office hereditary, largely independent of León. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> was a society of free peasant-soldiers and frontier towns, with its own customary law and a Romance dialect that became Castilian. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-garcia-of-castile">Sancho García</a> granted fueros to frontier settlers, and the counts fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> and his successors.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The murder of the young Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garcia-sanchez-of-castile">García Sánchez</a> in 1029 brought <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III of Navarre</a>, whose son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-leon">Ferdinand I</a> ruled it and became king of León in 1037. From 1065 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> was a kingdom in its own right under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ii-of-castile">Sancho II</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-castile.jpg" alt="County of Castile" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: drini · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Castile.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>850 CE – 1065 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Burgos</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fernán González, Sancho García, Ferdinand I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Castilian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Condado de Castilla</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=958&amp;focus=county-of-castile" aria-label="County of Castile on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4590 672 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Castile lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4910 928L4859 895M4910 928L4865 919M4910 928L4960 913M4910 928L4910 928M4910 928L4928 915"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4859 895h0M4865 919h0M4960 913h0M4910 928h0M4928 915h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4910 928h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4910 928h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Burgos. <a href="/atlas?year=958&amp;focus=county-of-castile">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
